                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “Every business signed the same page-and-a-half contract,” Sofía
said. “Boring. A one-time install, a free trial, a ‘community partner’
decal in the window.” She looked at me over the laptop. “Which of the
six businesses in a mile of here did not sign it?”
   She didn’t wait.
   “One. The café. I called. Mr. Rodríguez told their rep to take the
speaker back to whatever planet it came from. He plays his own re‐
cords.”
   The air in the room changed, the way it does right before something
lines up.
   “A control,” I said.
   Sofía’s face did something I’d never seen it do. She looked, for about
two seconds, completely and unfairly happy.
   “I’m in love with a coffee shop,” she said.



The part I hadn’t expected came about twenty minutes later, when
Sofía said, almost to herself, “There’s a thing I don’t understand.”
   She’d been following the registered-agent address, the one all three
shells share. It was lazy, she said, a lawyer’s habit, and people don’t
usually reuse something that traceable unless they’ve done it so long
they’ve stopped checking. She’d searched the address on its own, with
no company name, just the street and the suite, and gone backward.
   “It’s had other names,” she said. “Aperture isn’t new. The same ad‐
dress has held a data startup, a consultancy, and before that—” She
turned the screen. A scanned PDF, grainy, with a state seal and a
foundation’s letterhead. Quiet Hours Foundation. Early Calm Pilot: Final
Report. 2007. “A grant. Four elementary schools, all in this zip code. A
‘calm environment’ program. Ambient tones in the classrooms, plus a
screening tool.”
   I went still.
   “JC.” She said it gently. “You went to elementary school in this zip.”
   I remembered the speaker.




